    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1008">

                <pb facs="1041" n="921" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg921"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">823.01/79: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Peru</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Dearing</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-05-19T16:00:00-04:00">May 19, 1931—4
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>35. Your 224, May 15, 2 p.m. You may answer Peruvian Government’s note of March
                        12<anchor n="25" xml:id="d1008-f1"/> last and state that the change in
                    government in Peru will make no difference in the diplomatic relations between
                    the United States and Peru.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1008-f1" n="25" xml:id="d1008-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 116, March 13, noon, from the Ambassador in Peru, <ref
                        target="#pg912">p. 912</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
